ient to prevent the  surge that you're talking about  now certainly good so wearing mask  vaccination is is key keeping distance  is certainly key but this virus cannot  pretty much be stopped right now because  it's so infectious and nevertheless we  should try to continue to  reduce the number of contacts wearing  mask wherever possible for sure indoors  and and just trying to really um go  through this wave together in a very  respectful way wow this very cannot be  stopped right now that's pretty  remarkable to hear that i mean  why are some of the infection numbers  here in germany still lower than in  other european countries do you chalk  that up to um  you know the the masks in social  distancing here or other measures  nothing is difficult to say i don't  think that the numbers are um if you if  you're looking  across several weeks will will not be  that different than other surrounding  countries from from germany it certainly  started a bit a bit later we have seen  this also in the second wave but i don't  think there's a  a clear difference compared to other  countries  we'll have to leave it there tobias  court epidemiologist at the charite  hospital here in berlin thank you very  much  pleasure thanks for having me  well for more on this i'm joined now by  our political correspondent thomas  sparrow  thomas  we're seeing a record number of  infections here in germany driven by the  omicron variant of coronavirus how is  the country coping  it's in fact terry the third consecutive  day that germany registers  uh record in the number of infections  you mentioned already 90 000 over 90 000  new cases in the last 24 hours and that  trend is likely to continue the robert  koch institute the country's public  health institute has said that they  expect a strong increase in the number  of cases driven by the omicron variant  in the next few weeks and as you can  imagine this is a scenario that is  causing a lot of concern not only among  citizens in fact a n